# Photograph the whole prestige-class menu, so a check can read which classes
# the game offers a player.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Eight prestige classes in lib/prestige.irh are unfinished:
# each carries a "*** NOT FINISHED YET ***" banner and the same placeholder
# body -- d4 hit die, 12 mana, 1/4 defence, no class skills, no grants. They
# were still listed, so a player picked one and only then read "This prestige
# class is still under development and cannot be used." They now carry
# CF_PSEUDO, which every list-building site in the engine skips
# (src/Managers.cpp:2271, src/Create.cpp:306, src/Help.cpp:763, :789, :840,
# src/Skills.cpp:97). tools/check_prestige_hidden.sh reads this dump.
#
# HOW IT GETS THERE. Free Advancement is a wizard-mode switch, so the script
# enters wizard mode (lowercase w -- 'W' is a different keystroke, see
# TokenToKey in src/Wposix.cpp), turns the switch on, and then uses the
# character sheet's [G] to reach the three core levels that
# src/Managers.cpp:2252 demands before it will open the prestige list.
#
# NOTHING IS TAKEN. Pressing a menu letter TAKES the class (LMenu returns the
# moment its letter arrives, src/TextTerm.cpp:1217-1231), so this script only
# photographs the menu and presses ESC.
#
# SEED-INDEPENDENT for what it asserts: the menu is static text built from the
# module. The chargen it includes is seed-locked, which is why a seed is still
# passed.

@include chargen-rogue.keys

ESC             # dismiss the arrival-room description
w               # wizard mode
y               # "Enter wizard mode?" -- yes
w               # [w] Wizard Mode Switches
DOWN DOWN       # Cheat Death -> Auto-Identify Items -> Free Advancement
SPACE           # NO -> YES
ESC             # leave the options manager

d               # character sheet
G G G           # three core levels; the prestige list refuses fewer

@choose "Prestige Classes"
@expect "Choose a class:"
@dump:prestige-menu

ESC
@quit
